WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  novak djokovic has repeatedly spoken out  in the past two weeks  against the decision by the australian  open organisers  to quarantine players ahead of the  tournament but in front of the almost  seven and a half thousand fans allowed  in to watch at melbourne park  with trophy in hand it seemed as if all  was forgiven  look there are a lot of mixed feelings  about what has happened in  the last month or so with tennis players  coming to australia  but i think when we draw a line in the  end it was a successful tournament for  organizers djokovic hadn't lost in 17  appearances in semi-finals and finals at  the australian open  his opponent daniel medvedev hadn't lost  to anyone since october  but the world number one showed his  intent from the very start  in the second set the pressure proved  too much for the russian  nine grand slams in australia 18 total  is amazing and probably it's not your  last one so i mean  i have no words to say uh it's a matter  of time  when you're going to hold the grand slam  that's for sure  if you don't mind waiting few more years  djokovic has won six of the last ten  majors and is now just  two wins away from joining roger federer  and rafael nadal  with the men's all-time overall record  of 20 grand slam titles  the one in melbourne guarantees he'll  stay world number one until at least  march 8th  that'll be 311 weeks in the top spot  and that breaks another of federer's  records  paul vanderworth al jazeera

